      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "Cardiovascular mortality and morbidity were assessed, after a mean follow-up period of 5 years, in an unselected series of 159 adults presenting with the nephrotic syndrome between 1972 and 1975. 60% of the deaths were attributed to terminal renal failure, and the incidence of deaths from ischaemic heart-disease (IHD) was not significantly above normal. The proportion of patients experiencing angina and intermittent claudication and the prevalence of ischaemic electrocardiographic changes did not differ significantly from those of a London control population. At follow-up, hypertension was significantly more common (p less than 0.001) in male nephrotic patients than in controls. Earlier reports of a greatly increased incidence of IHD in unselected patients with the nephrotic syndrome were not confirmed. Routine treatment of hyperlipidaemia in the nephrotic syndrome is not, therefore, recommended."},
